      "dom_id": "question-4776",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=-{x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
